From 84089ca48a08b258c2c784ccf01976be020a0c08 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Yahia <yahia.mostafa.elsayed@gmail.com>
Date: Wed, 14 Oct 2026 22:37:28 +0300
Subject: [PATCH 1/4] Take the guest's page size per device, for arm64 guests
 on x86_64

The library assumed the guest's pages are the size of its own build's
architecture (APV_PAGE_SHIFT), as QEMU only offered the device to guests
of the host's architecture. An arm64 guest (16 KiB pages) running under
TCG on an x86_64 host needs the guest's size instead.

- RvgDeviceConfig.guest_page_shift and RvgIOSurfaceDeviceConfig.
  guest_page_shift, appended under the sized-struct rules: 0 is the
  library's own architecture, 12 and 14 are accepted, anything else is
  RVG_ERR_INVALID_ARGUMENT; callers passing the earlier sizes read 0.
  Rust: DeviceConfig::guest_page_shift, IoSurfaceDeviceConfig::
  guest_page_shift.
- A GuestPages value carries the device's pages through core: the root
  node, FIFO page lists, task roots, page-table walks, the range tracker,
  object lists, ICB and buffer pages, the device-info and display pages
  (the colour-matrix page check is now a runtime check), the EFI
  framebuffer, memory-map alignment and isolated GPU tasks.
- The IOSurface mapper's table geometry, descriptor pages, entry decode
  and page counts use guest pages; host remapping stays in host pages, a
  16 KiB guest page mapping as four 4 KiB host pages.
- Tests for shift 14 on an x86_64 build, including the C API end to end;
  header regenerated, docs/api.md updated.

Rebased onto upstream 1fe0acb6, which added RvgDeviceConfig.dirty_source
at offset 64 (size 72): guest_page_shift stays at offset 60, in the padding
before it, so dirty_source keeps upstream's offset and a host built against
the earlier 64-byte vphone header still reads its shift. docs/api.md and the
layout test name both. The C API's video tests fill in guest_page_shift in
their RvgIOSurfaceDeviceConfig too.

